{"person_id":"gunnar-nordahl","name":"Gunnar Nordahl","cohort_group":"Athletes","category":"Footballer (striker)","birth_year":1921,"age_at_milestone":23,"milestone_by_age_26":"Won three Swedish championships with IFK Norrköping (1945, 1946, 1947), twice finished as Allsvenskan top scorer (1945, 1946), and was awarded the Swedish Guldbollen (Footballer of the Year) in 1947, all before turning 26.","early_history_summary":"Nordahl began playing senior football at Hörnefors IF at age 16 in 1937, scoring 68 goals in 41 games. He moved to Degerfors IF in the top-flight Allsvenskan in 1940 at age 19, then to IFK Norrköping in 1944 where he became the league's dominant striker, winning four consecutive Swedish championships and earning a national team call-up in 1942.","primary_leverage_engine":"Physical talent and goal-scoring instinct","normalized_primary_engine":"Physical / athletic talent","secondary_engine":"Early specialization","started_serious_reps_before_20_score":1,"prior_reps_score":2,"scarce_skill_depth_score":2,"native_distribution_score":0,"elite_ecosystem_network_score":1,"complementary_team_score":1,"structural_wave_score":1,"concentration_intensity_score":1,"capital_safety_score":0,"domain_proximity_score":1,"total_leverage_score":10,"success_tier":2,"leverage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","family_context_summary":"Nordahl came from Hörnefors, a small Swedish town. His brothers Bertil and Knut also played professional football, suggesting a footballing family, but specific family financial background is not documented.","parent_family_domain_summary":"Not documented in reviewed sources.","early_family_financial_platform_support_score":0,"parent_family_domain_advantage_score":0,"inherited_audience_business_network_score":0,"elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score":1,"frontier_geography_ecosystem_score":0,"rare_early_tools_facilities_score":0,"dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score":0,"exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score":1,"early_online_platform_community_score":0,"direct_customer_domain_exposure_score":1,"prodigy_physical_edge_score":1,"adversity_constraint_catalyst_score":0,"inherited_access_stack_count":4,"exceptional_inherited_access_count":0,"all_documented_early_condition_count":4,"primary_early_advantage_archetype":"Elite performance pipeline","primary_early_advantage_tags":"Allsvenskan pipeline, early professional debut, physical goal-scoring talent, footballing brothers","evidence_summary":"Nordahl began playing senior football at 16 in 1937 and was in the top-flight Allsvenskan by 19. At IFK Norrköping from 1944, he won three Swedish championships before age 26 (1945, 1946, 1947), was Allsvenskan top scorer multiple times, and won the 1947 Guldbollen as Sweden's best player. He was a Sweden international from 1942, scoring 43 goals in 33 caps. No specific family financial or domain advantage is documented, though his brothers also played professionally.","early_advantage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","source_count":3,"primary_source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunnar_Nordahl","source_urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunnar_Nordahl","https://www.transfermarkt.com/gunnar-nordahl/erfolge/spieler/140644","https://sok.se/idrottare/idrottare/g/gunnar-nordahl.html"],"annotation_status":"subagent_researched_beta","source_audit_status":"not_independently_audited","data_version":"0.2.0-subagent-beta","eligibility_status":"age_26_eligible"}
{"person_id":"rik-van-looy","name":"Rik Van Looy","cohort_group":"Athletes","category":"Cyclist (road racing)","birth_year":1933,"age_at_milestone":19,"milestone_by_age_26":"Won Olympic gold in the team road race (1952), the Belgian amateur road championship (1952), bronze at the World Championship amateur road race (1953), and multiple professional classics including Milan-San Remo (1958), Gent-Wevelgem (1956, 1957), and Tour of Flanders (1959), all before age 26.","early_history_summary":"Van Looy was fascinated by cycling from childhood, working as a paper boy and riding a heavy bicycle daily. He started racing at 15, won 16 races in a row the following year, and turned professional at 19 in 1953 after winning 150 amateur races and two Belgian national amateur titles. He quickly became one of the dominant professional classics riders of the late 1950s.","primary_leverage_engine":"Physical talent and sprint power","normalized_primary_engine":"Physical / athletic talent","secondary_engine":"Early specialization","started_serious_reps_before_20_score":1,"prior_reps_score":2,"scarce_skill_depth_score":2,"native_distribution_score":0,"elite_ecosystem_network_score":1,"complementary_team_score":1,"structural_wave_score":1,"concentration_intensity_score":1,"capital_safety_score":0,"domain_proximity_score":1,"total_leverage_score":10,"success_tier":2,"leverage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","family_context_summary":"Van Looy was born in Grobbendonk, Antwerp Province, Belgium. He worked as a paper boy before age 13, riding a heavy bicycle daily, which laid the foundation for his career. No specific family financial or cycling background is documented.","parent_family_domain_summary":"Not documented in reviewed sources.","early_family_financial_platform_support_score":0,"parent_family_domain_advantage_score":0,"inherited_audience_business_network_score":0,"elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score":1,"frontier_geography_ecosystem_score":1,"rare_early_tools_facilities_score":0,"dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score":0,"exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score":0,"early_online_platform_community_score":0,"direct_customer_domain_exposure_score":1,"prodigy_physical_edge_score":1,"adversity_constraint_catalyst_score":1,"inherited_access_stack_count":5,"exceptional_inherited_access_count":0,"all_documented_early_condition_count":5,"primary_early_advantage_archetype":"Self-created domain repetition","primary_early_advantage_tags":"early cycling immersion, Belgian cycling heartland, sprint power, self-driven training, working-class background","evidence_summary":"Van Looy grew up in Grobbendonk, Belgium, and was fascinated by cycling from childhood. He started racing at 15, won 16 races the following year, and accumulated 150 amateur wins before turning professional at 19 in 1953. By age 26, he had won Olympic gold (1952), multiple professional classics including Milan-San Remo (1958) and Gent-Wevelgem (1956-57), and Grand Tour stages. His unprecedented sprint power and attacking style made him the dominant classics rider of the late 1950s. No specific family advantages are documented beyond his working-class upbringing in the Belgian cycling heartland.","early_advantage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","source_count":3,"primary_source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rik_Van_Looy","source_urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rik_Van_Looy","https://www.olympedia.org/athletes/17008","https://cyclinglegends.co.uk/blogs/long-reads/an-audience-with-the-emperor"],"annotation_status":"subagent_researched_beta","source_audit_status":"not_independently_audited","data_version":"0.2.0-subagent-beta","eligibility_status":"age_26_eligible"}
